# Practical 8: Complete CI/CD Workflow Using GitHub Actions

**Student:** Tshering Tenzin  
**ID:** 02250376  

## Objective
Implement a complete CI/CD pipeline using GitHub Actions that automatically builds, tests, and deploys a Flask application whenever code is pushed to GitHub.

## Repository Structure

.
├── .github/workflows/ci-cd.yml
├── app.py
├── Dockerfile
├── requirements.txt
└── .gitignore

text

## Workflow File (`.github/workflows/ci-cd.yml`)

```yaml
name: CI/CD Pipeline

on:
  push:
    branches: [ main, master ]
  pull_request:
    branches: [ main, master ]

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with: { python-version: '3.11' }
      - run: pip install -r requirements.txt
      - run: docker build -t my-flask-app:${{ github.sha }} .
  
  test:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - run: pip install -r requirements.txt pytest
      - run: echo "def test_app(): assert True" > test_app.py && pytest test_app.py -v
      - run: docker build -t test-image . && docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name test-container test-image && sleep 3 && curl -f http://localhost:5000 && docker stop test-container
  
  deploy:
    needs: test
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    if: github.ref == 'refs/heads/main'
    steps:
      - run: echo "Deploying to production (simulated)..."
      - run: echo " Deployment successful"
```
Execution Results
Workflow triggered on git push to main

All jobs (build, test, deploy) completed successfully

Console logs show green checkmarks on GitHub Actions interface